# BSA Model H, 557 cc side-valve single, with the BSA Type 1 sidecar.
#
# The 1920s outfit: BSA's own sidecar, sold with the 4¼ hp model from the 1921
# season. The 1923 range lists the 4¼ hp as 85 x 97 mm (557 cc), single cylinder
# four-stroke side-valve, drip-feed lubrication, BSA carburettor, chain-driven
# magneto, three-speed gearbox with clutch and kick-starter, chain drive, and
# 26 x 2½ in tyres; solo £75, with sidecar £95. Model H is the all-chain model,
# Model K the chain-cum-belt version. The frame carries integral sidecar lugs.
# https://www.cybermotorcycle.com/marques/bsa/bsa-1923-models.htm
#
# The 1921 season introduced the redesigned 4¼ hp engine, gearbox and clutch and
# a new sidecar built for the machine: a triangulated chassis with a double
# tubular axle, two side members converging forward, a single coil spring at the
# front of the body and a single eleven-leaf cross spring at the rear. The body
# has a deep back and cushions and a locker with a flat top for luggage.
# The Motor Cycle, 25 November 1920, via
# https://cybermotorcycle.com/marques/bsa/bsa-1921-models.htm
# The photographed machine is a 1923 Model H with the BSA Type 1 sidecar.
# https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/BSA:_Motorcycles
#
# Dimensions: overall 2.28 m long x 1.44 m wide x 1.15 m high including the
# mudguards, measured from the reference photographs; motorcycle wheelbase about
# 1.40 m, wheels 26 in with 2.5 in section. No published overall figure was found
# for the outfit, so these are measurements, not maker's figures.
#
# Colours: BSA tanks were green in the flat-tank years; the 1926 season changed
# the tank from green to black. The photographed 1923 outfit has a green tank
# with gold lining, a black frame and guards, and a green sidecar body with a
# gold coachline. Green and black are therefore the two modelled colourways.
# https://bsaoc.org/swe/bsa_bladet/bladet20102.pdf
# https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/BSA:_Motorcycles
#
# Colour shares and weight: no model-level outfit counts exist for the 1920s.
# Motorcycles outnumbered cars for most of the decade (278,600 in 1920, 491,438
# in 1925, 690,672 in 1928) and outfits were the characteristic Very-low
# motorised vehicle; the 1936 measured peak share of the motorcycle parc was
# 24.8%. Against the 1925 anchor (Ford Model T, 500 on the 2026-11-15 scale) the
# outfit is set at 180 in total: green 110, black 70. Provisional pending the
# central rebase.
#
# Speed 64 km/h (40 mph): a game-balance figure for a 557 cc side-valve outfit.
# Comfort 20/180, below the post-war M21 outfit's 25/190: rigid frame, sprung
# saddle only, and a sidecar on leaf springs.
#
# Dates describe when outfits were seen regularly on UK roads. The 4¼ hp with
# the BSA sidecar ran from the 1921 season; flat-tank models were superseded in
# the later 1920s and outfits faded through the early 1930s.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/bsa-557-combo.blend in the blends repository.
# References: citycars/references/bsa-557-combo/ (gitignored research copies).
# Image credits: 1923 Model H with Type 1 sidecar, Grace's Guide contributor
# photographs; 1921 4¼ hp side view, The Motor Cycle via Sheldon's EMU.
# Stage B note: +0.2973 pak units applied after preparation so the motorcycle,
# not the combined bounding box, sits at the tile centre.
#
# Rider and sidecar passenger added 2026-11-20 in Blender 4.5. One seated rider on
# the motorcycle and one adult in the open Type 1 chair; both wear period clothing
# and a black helmet. The figures are a stylised low-poly reconstruction fitted to
# the master: no photograph of a rider on this exact chair exists in the pack. The
# object names, dates and colour lists are unchanged.

